"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/MarineYachtRadar/mayara-server-signalk-plugin/commits/394f36d522d39df1e3455820e1bb40ec5e1561b8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-09T18:25:15Z","email":"86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dirk Wahrheit"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-09T18:25:15Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(gui): open the radar GUI directly on mayara-server by default (#103)\n\n* feat(gui): add option to open the radar GUI directly on mayara-server\n\nThe GUI is reached through this plugin''s reverse proxy, which keeps the\nbrowser on the Signal K port so HTTPS works and mayara-server''s own port can\nstay closed. On installations where that indirection is unwanted, there was\nno way to opt out.\n\n`directGuiUrl` sends the browser to mayara-server''s port instead. The AIS\noverlay is unaffected: mayara-server subscribes to `vessels.*` on its\nupstream Signal K connection and serves the targets from its own store, so\nthe direct GUI shows the same vessels.\n\nThe hostname comes from the request rather than the configured `host`. In\ncontainer mode `host` is `localhost`, which names the machine running the\nbrowser, not the one running mayara — building the URL from it would send\nevery remote browser to itself.\n\nThe splash page now resolves its target from /api/gui-url instead of a\nbuild-time constant, falling back to the proxy path if that request fails,\nand offers a plain link under <noscript> so a client without scripting is\nnot left on a page that never redirects.\n\n* feat(gui): default to opening the radar GUI directly on mayara-server\n\nFlips `directGuiUrl` to on, so the radar icon goes straight to\nmayara-server''s port unless the operator opts back into the proxy.\n\nThis changes behaviour on upgrade, not only for new installs: `start()`\nmerges SCHEMA_DEFAULTS under the stored config, so a configuration saved\nbefore this field existed adopts the new default. Installations that need\nthe proxy — mayara-server''s port closed to the network, or Signal K served\nover TLS with the radar session expected to be encrypted too — must disable\nthe setting explicitly. Covered by a test so the migration is deliberate.\n\nAlso corrects the transport wording in both the README and the schema\ndescription. The GUI is reached by a top-level navigation, not a subresource\nload, so plain HTTP is not mixed content; the actual consequence is an\nunencrypted radar session even when Signal K itself uses HTTPS.\n\n* test(gui-url): stop the first plugin before starting the second\n\nloadPlugin() calls vi.resetModules(), so overlapping instances risk\ncross-contamination between the two IPv6 assertions.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","tree":{"sha":"b1bcc3597d887a4cbd0ce93d5940f8180a4c139a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/MarineYachtRadar/mayara-server-signalk-plugin/git/trees/b1bcc3597d887a4cbd0ce93d5940f8180a4c139a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/MarineYachtRadar/mayara-server-signalk-plugin/git/commits/394f36d522d39df1e3455820e1bb40ec5e1561b8","verification":{"payload":"tree b1bcc3597d887a4cbd0ce93d5940f8180a4c139a\nparent 45969bf0eb6d9261d90e2dd604fdebd1efe78fb2\nauthor Dirk Wahrheit <86628605+dirkwa@users.noreply.github.com> 1786299915 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786299915 +1200\n\nfeat(gui): open the radar GUI directly on mayara-server by default (#103)\n\n* feat(gui): add option to open the radar GUI directly on mayara-server\n\nThe GUI is reached through this plugin''s reverse proxy, which keeps the\nbrowser on the Signal K port so HTTPS works and mayara-server''s own port can\nstay closed. On installations where that indirection is unwanted, there was\nno way to opt out.\n\n`directGuiUrl` sends the browser to mayara-server''s port instead. The AIS\noverlay is unaffected: mayara-server subscribes to `vessels.*` on its\nupstream Signal K connection and serves the targets from its own store, so\nthe direct GUI shows the same vessels.\n\nThe hostname comes from the request rather than the configured `host`. In\ncontainer mode `host` is `localhost`, which names the machine running the\nbrowser, not the one running mayara — building the URL from it would send\nevery remote browser to itself.\n\nThe splash page now resolves its target from /api/gui-url instead of a\nbuild-time constant, falling back to the proxy path if that request fails,\nand offers a plain link under <noscript> so a client without scripting is\nnot left on a page that never redirects.\n\n* feat(gui): default to opening the radar GUI directly on mayara-server\n\nFlips `directGuiUrl` to on, so the radar icon goes straight to\nmayara-server''s port unless the operator opts back into the proxy.\n\nThis changes behaviour on upgrade, not only for new installs: `start()`\nmerges SCHEMA_DEFAULTS under the stored config, so a configuration saved\nbefore this field existed adopts the new default. Installations that need\nthe proxy — mayara-server''s port closed to the network, or Signal K served\nover TLS with the radar session expected to be encrypted too — must disable\nthe setting explicitly. Covered by a test so the migration is deliberate.\n\nAlso corrects the transport wording in both the README and the schema\ndescription. The GUI is reached by a top-level navigation, not a subresource\nload, so plain HTTP is not mixed content; the actual consequence is an\nunencrypted radar session even when Signal K itself uses HTTPS.\n\n* test(gui-url): stop the first plugin before starting the second\n\nloadPlugin() calls vi.resetModules(), so overlapping instances risk\ncross-contamination between the two IPv6 assertions.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: dirkwa <dirkwahrtheit@gmail.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqeMYLCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAArSoQADPIWT5PIx7URtIm4fWHOScf\nKI0Sv9jxuZYYw6htPNtWIU6OTwfIYME3dj5MZ3KJ5+HOtORlSP36D/P5W1qMoX3A\nAQj